Extend the calibration combo set with 5 more variants and let the
test pick the winner. Findings (now recorded in PLANTARD_NOTES.md):

  - HAWK's R is 2^32 mod q, not 2^16 — initial design note was off
    by one Montgomery factor. Plantard's natural 2^{-32} output
    factor therefore matches HAWK's scaling exactly; no twiddle
    table re-baking is needed at the math level.

  - v6+encId (= Zq(montyred) inlined, identity twiddle encoding):
    matches mq18433_montymul on all 4096 random (a, b) pairs. This
    is the spec-correct reference behaviour we must reproduce.

  - v8+encId (Plantard form with HAWK's Q0I, unsigned shifts,
    qa=+1): misses 313/4096 = 7.6% always by exactly 1. The
    rounding is applied at a different point than montyred's '+1
    at end', and the algebra of the disagreement is now documented.

  - All other tried combos (Plantard with the wrong Qinv sign,
    or any non-identity pre-multiplication of the twiddle by Qinv)
    mismatch on every input.

Spec-adherence consequence (also recorded in the notes): HAWK
signatures encode NTT-domain coefficients byte-for-byte, so the
forthcoming M4 asm port has to reproduce Zq(montyred) exactly --
which costs one extra instruction per coefficient (3 cycles vs
Plantard's bare-minimum 2) but keeps KAT byte equality. The packed
double-butterfly structure (4 instructions per 2 coefficients) is
still a clear win over the ~6-7 cycle pure-C inner kernel. The
existing GM[] / iGM[] tables in modq.h feed the asm unchanged.

Next commit: plant_18433.S implementing the locked-down reducer.
